.Dd November 11, 2026
.Dt FUTEX 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm futex ,
.Nm futex_waitv ,
.Nm futex_wake ,
.Nm futex_wait ,
.Nm futex_requeue ,
.Nm set_robust_list ,
.Nm get_robust_list
.Nd wait on a word of memory and wake its waiters
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In linux/futex.h
.In sys/syscall.h
.In unistd.h
.Ft long
.Fn syscall "SYS_futex" "uint32_t *uaddr" "int op" "uint32_t val" "const struct timespec *timeout" "uint32_t *uaddr2" "uint32_t val3"
.Ft long
.Fn syscall "SYS_futex_waitv" "struct futex_waitv *waiters" "unsigned int nr_futexes" "unsigned int flags" "const struct __kernel_timespec *timeout" "clockid_t clockid"
.Ft long
.Fn syscall "SYS_futex_wake" "uint32_t *uaddr" "unsigned long mask" "int nr" "unsigned int flags"
.Ft long
.Fn syscall "SYS_futex_wait" "uint32_t *uaddr" "unsigned long val" "unsigned long mask" "unsigned int flags" "const struct __kernel_timespec *timeout" "clockid_t clockid"
.Ft long
.Fn syscall "SYS_futex_requeue" "struct futex_waitv *waiters" "unsigned int flags" "int nr_wake" "int nr_requeue"
.Ft long
.Fn syscall "SYS_set_robust_list" "struct robust_list_head *head" "size_t len"
.Ft long
.Fn syscall "SYS_get_robust_list" "int tid" "struct robust_list_head **head" "size_t *len"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm ,
number 240 on i386 and 202 on x86-64, lets a process sleep until the
32-bit word at
.Fa uaddr
changes, without spinning, and lets another wake it.
C libraries build their locks, condition variables and one-time
initialization on it.
The address must be aligned to four bytes.
A futex is named by the caller's address space and
.Fa uaddr ,
so the threads of a process, and processes that
.Xr vfork 2
or
.Xr clone 2
with
.Dv CLONE_VM
made to share one, name it alike.
A word in a shared mapping, a file in
.Pa /dev/shm
or shared anonymous memory, is named instead by its page's frame and its
offset in it, which every process mapping the page names alike, unless the
operation has
.Dv FUTEX_PRIVATE_FLAG ;
a mutex made
.Dv PTHREAD_PROCESS_SHARED
is one such word.
.Pp
The operations are:
.Bl -tag -width FUTEX_WAIT_BITSET
.It Dv FUTEX_WAIT
If the word holds
.Fa val ,
sleep until a wake, a signal, or the relative
.Fa timeout ,
measured on the monotonic clock; a NULL
.Fa timeout
waits without a limit.
The comparison and the start of the sleep are one step, so a wake that
follows the comparison is not lost.
.It Dv FUTEX_WAIT_BITSET
The same, with
.Fa val3
as a nonzero set of bits a wake must share, and
.Fa timeout
an absolute time on the monotonic clock, or on the real-time clock with
.Dv FUTEX_CLOCK_REALTIME .
.It Dv FUTEX_WAKE
Wake up to
.Fa val
waiters on the word, and return how many were woken.
A
.Fa val
of 0, or one that is negative as an int, wakes one, as on Linux.
.It Dv FUTEX_WAKE_BITSET
The same, waking only waiters whose bits meet the nonzero
.Fa val3 .
.It Dv FUTEX_REQUEUE
Wake up to
.Fa val
waiters on the word and move up to the fourth argument's value more to the
futex at
.Fa uaddr2 ,
where a wake of that word wakes them, and return how many were woken and
moved.
A negative count fails with
.Er EINVAL .
.It Dv FUTEX_CMP_REQUEUE
The same, while the word holds
.Fa val3 ,
compared in the same step; otherwise
.Er EAGAIN .
.It Dv FUTEX_WAKE_OP
Change the word at
.Fa uaddr2
by the operation encoded in
.Fa val3 ,
in one atomic step, wake up to
.Fa val
waiters on the word at
.Fa uaddr ,
and, when the encoded comparison holds for the second word's old value,
up to the fourth argument's value of its waiters; return how many were
woken.
.Fa val3
has the operation in bits 28 to 30,
.Dv FUTEX_OP_SET ,
.Dv FUTEX_OP_ADD ,
.Dv FUTEX_OP_OR ,
.Dv FUTEX_OP_ANDN
or
.Dv FUTEX_OP_XOR ,
0 to 4, with bit 31,
.Dv FUTEX_OP_OPARG_SHIFT ,
to take the argument as a bit number; the comparison in bits 24 to 27,
.Dv FUTEX_OP_CMP_EQ ,
.Dv _NE ,
.Dv _LT ,
.Dv _LE ,
.Dv _GT
or
.Dv _GE ,
0 to 5; the signed 12-bit argument in bits 12 to 23 and the signed 12-bit
comparand in bits 0 to 11.
An unknown operation fails with
.Er ENOSYS
and leaves the word; an unknown comparison fails with
.Er ENOSYS
after the change, as on Linux.
.It Dv FUTEX_LOCK_PI
Acquire the PI mutex, sleeping behind its owner if necessary.
The timeout is absolute on the real-time clock.
.It Dv FUTEX_LOCK_PI2
Acquire the PI mutex with an absolute monotonic timeout, or real time with
.Dv FUTEX_CLOCK_REALTIME .
.It Dv FUTEX_TRYLOCK_PI
Acquire without sleeping; a busy mutex fails with
.Er EAGAIN .
.It Dv FUTEX_UNLOCK_PI
Release a mutex owned by the caller, passing it to the highest-priority
waiter, with equal priorities in arrival order.
.It Dv FUTEX_WAIT_REQUEUE_PI
Compare the condition word with
.Fa val
and wait to be transferred to the distinct PI mutex at
.Fa uaddr2 .
The timeout is absolute on the monotonic clock, or real time with
.Dv FUTEX_CLOCK_REALTIME .
.It Dv FUTEX_CMP_REQUEUE_PI
Require
.Fa val
1, compare the condition word with
.Fa val3 ,
and move up to one plus the fourth argument's value waiters to the PI mutex at
.Fa uaddr2 .
Each must have used
.Dv FUTEX_WAIT_REQUEUE_PI
with that destination.
The first waiter acquires a free mutex immediately; others wait behind its owner.
The result counts transferred waiters.
.El
.Pp
A deadline is rounded up to whole 1 ms ticks, counted from the tick the
monotonic clock is in, a tick whose interrupt has not yet been taken
included, and the tick in progress is not counted, so a wait never ends
before the time asked by that clock.
It is fixed when the call begins, so the time taken to bring in the
word's page counts against it.
.Pp
A signal the process acts on ends a wait.
A wait without a timeout is restarted when no handler runs, as after a
stop and a continue, or when the handler that runs has
.Dv SA_RESTART ,
as
.Xr sigaction 2
describes.
A wait with a timeout fails with
.Er EINTR
after a handler, whatever its flags, and when no handler runs it goes on
through
.Xr restart_syscall 2
to the deadline tick fixed at the first call, as Linux's does.
On i686 i386's time64 form, number 422, is not implemented; x86-64 has no
such form of its own, and takes 422 from an i386 program, below.
.Ss Priority inheritance
A PI mutex word is 0 while unlocked; its low 30 bits hold its owner's
thread ID while locked.
.Dv FUTEX_WAITERS
requires an unlock through the kernel and
.Dv FUTEX_OWNER_DIED
records an owner's death.
Waiters donate their effective real-time priority to the owner, through
any chain of owners blocked on other PI mutexes.
A donation leaves the base policy and reported priority unchanged.
Unlock, timeout and signal remove the donation.
The caller must not mix ordinary and PI waiters on one mutex word.
.Pp
PI acquisition restarts after a signal handler even without
.Dv SA_RESTART ,
keeping its absolute deadline.
A requeue wait has the same restart before transfer; after transfer a
signal returns
.Er EAGAIN .
A completed handoff wins over a simultaneous signal or deadline.
When a thread ends, contended PI locks pass to their top waiters and the
new owners repair their words before returning.
Robust PI locks report
.Dv FUTEX_OWNER_DIED
also when no waiter was present at exit.
.Ss Waiting on several words
.Nm futex_waitv ,
number 449 on i386 and on x86-64, sleeps while each of
.Fa nr_futexes
words, 1 to 128, holds its value, until a wake of any of them, a signal
or the deadline, and returns the index in
.Fa waiters
of the futex that was woken; when wakes came for several before the
caller ran, the highest such index.
Wine waits on it for the events and mutexes of Proton's fsync.
Each entry is
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct futex_waitv {
	uint64_t val;		/* the value the word must hold */
	uint64_t uaddr;		/* the word's address */
	uint32_t flags;		/* FUTEX2_SIZE_U32 [| FUTEX2_PRIVATE] */
	uint32_t __reserved;	/* 0 */
};
.Ed
.Pp
with the same layout on i386 and x86-64.
.Dv FUTEX2_SIZE_U32 ,
2, is the only size taken;
.Dv FUTEX2_PRIVATE ,
128, names the word as
.Dv FUTEX_PRIVATE_FLAG
does, and without it a word in a shared mapping is named by its page as
above, so a
.Dv FUTEX_WAKE
of
.Nm futex
by another process wakes it.
The words are compared and the waiters filed as one step, as for
.Dv FUTEX_WAIT ,
so a wake that follows the comparison is not lost; a word that does not
hold its value ends the call with
.Er EAGAIN
and none of them is waited on.
.Fa flags
must be 0.
.Fa timeout ,
when not NULL, is an absolute time of 64-bit fields on both
architectures, on the clock
.Fa clockid
names,
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC
or
.Dv CLOCK_REALTIME .
Negative seconds, or nanoseconds outside 0 to 999999999, fail with
.Er EINVAL ;
a 32-bit caller's nanoseconds have their high word ignored, as Linux's
.Fn get_timespec64
ignores it.
A signal that ends the wait has it restarted when no handler runs or the
handler has
.Dv SA_RESTART ,
since the deadline is absolute.
.Ss The futex2 calls
.Nm futex_wake ,
.Nm futex_wait
and
.Nm futex_requeue ,
numbers 454, 455 and 456 on i386 and on x86-64, are Linux 6.7's calls of
the same operations as
.Dv FUTEX_WAKE_BITSET ,
.Dv FUTEX_WAIT_BITSET
and
.Dv FUTEX_CMP_REQUEUE ,
with the flags of a
.Vt struct futex_waitv
entry in place of the operation's:
.Dv FUTEX2_SIZE_U32 ,
with
.Dv FUTEX2_PRIVATE
or without.
Any other flag, the other sizes and
.Dv FUTEX2_NUMA ,
4, among them, fails with
.Er EINVAL ,
as on Linux 6.12.
They share the queues of
.Nm futex
and
.Nm futex_waitv ,
so a
.Nm futex_wake
wakes a
.Dv FUTEX_WAIT
waiter and a
.Dv FUTEX_WAKE
a
.Nm futex_wait
one.
.Pp
.Nm futex_wake
wakes up to
.Fa nr
waiters on the word whose bits meet
.Fa mask ,
none for an
.Fa nr
of 0 and one for a negative one.
.Nm futex_wait
sleeps while the word holds
.Fa val ,
until a wake whose bits meet
.Fa mask ,
a signal or the absolute
.Fa timeout
on
.Fa clockid ,
which it reads as
.Nm futex_waitv
does; a signal has it restarted as
.Nm futex_waitv
is.
A
.Fa mask
of 0, or a value or mask past 32 bits, fails with
.Er EINVAL .
.Nm futex_requeue
takes two entries, the futex to wake and its value and the futex to move
waiters to, each with its own flags, and, while the first word holds the
first entry's value, wakes up to
.Fa nr_wake
waiters of it and moves up to
.Fa nr_requeue
more to the second;
.Fa flags
must be 0.
.Ss Robust lists
.Nm set_robust_list ,
number 311 on i386 and 273 on x86-64, records
.Fa head
as the calling thread's robust list, the list of robust mutexes it holds
that GNU libc keeps;
.Fa len
must be the size of the head, 12 on i386 and 24 on x86-64.
The head has the first entry, the offset from an entry to its futex word,
and the entry being taken or released at the moment, if any.
When the thread ends, and when it calls
.Xr execve 2 ,
the kernel walks the list, at most 2048 entries, and gives each word that
still holds the thread's ID
.Dv FUTEX_OWNER_DIED
in its place, keeping
.Dv FUTEX_WAITERS ,
and wakes one waiter when the word has waiters; the next owner then
learns from
.Er EOWNERDEAD
that the lock's state may be half made.
The entry the thread was taking or giving back is handled last, the same
way, except that a word of it with no owner, as an unlock the thread
stored and did not yet wake leaves it, has one waiter woken and is left
as it is.
A priority-inheritance entry, one with the low bit set, gets no such
wake.
An
.Xr execve 2
by a thread other than the first of its process walks the list before
the thread takes the process ID, so the ID it looks for is the one the
thread locked the words with; Linux walks it after, and looks for the
process ID.
.Nm get_robust_list ,
number 312 on i386 and 274 on x86-64, stores the list's head and the
head's size for the thread whose ID in the caller's PID namespace is
.Fa tid ,
0 for the caller.
Another thread's needs a thread of the caller's process, a privileged
caller
.Pq see Xr capget 2 ,
or an effective user ID equal to the thread's real or effective one and
a thread that is not privileged.
Linux applies its
.Xr ptrace 2
access check with the caller's real IDs
.Pq Dv PTRACE_MODE_READ_REALCREDS :
for a thread of another process and a caller without
.Dv CAP_SYS_PTRACE ,
it compares them with the thread's real, effective and saved user and
group IDs and asks for a dumpable process, where this kernel compares
the effective user ID alone and does not ask for one.
.Pp
On x86-64
.Nm futex
is call 202, with a timespec of 64-bit fields, and the robust list's head,
which
.Nm set_robust_list
(273) takes, is 24 bytes.
An i386 program on the x86-64 kernel
.Pq see Xr ia32 7
has i386's numbers: 240 with the 32-bit timespec, 422 with the 64-bit
one, whose nanoseconds' high word is ignored as Linux ignores it, and a
robust list of 32-bit words with a head of 12 bytes.
That list is kept beside the thread's native one, as Linux keeps both, and
the kernel walks both when the thread ends;
.Nm get_robust_list
by
.Ql int $0x80
reports it, its head and size as 32-bit words.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
A wait returns 0 when a wake ended it, a wake the number it woke, and a
requeue the number it woke and moved.
.Nm futex_waitv
returns the index of the woken futex.
The raw call returns a negative error number on failure;
.Fn syscall
instead returns -1 and sets
.Va errno .
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Pa tests/futex2probe.c
wakes a classic waiter with
.Nm futex_wake
and a
.Nm futex_wait
waiter with
.Dv FUTEX_WAKE ,
moves waiters with
.Nm futex_requeue
and
.Dv FUTEX_CMP_REQUEUE ,
changes and wakes with
.Dv FUTEX_WAKE_OP ,
and checks the refusals Linux 6.12 makes.
.Pa tests/musl/clonemusl.c
waits on a word that holds another value, waits out relative and
absolute deadlines, wakes an address with no waiters, and has a handler
with
.Dv SA_RESTART
end a timed wait with
.Er EINTR .
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width ETIMEDOUT
.It Er EDEADLK
A PI lock is already owned by the caller, or a blocking acquisition would
close a chain of blocked owners.
.It Er EAGAIN
A PI trylock is busy, a requeue wait was woken before transfer or interrupted
after it, a comparison or atomic exchange raced, or the word did not hold
.Fa val ,
or a word of
.Nm futex_waitv ,
.Nm futex_wait
or
.Nm futex_requeue
did not hold its value.
.It Er EFAULT
.Fa uaddr ,
.Fa timeout ,
.Fa waiters
or a word it names is not readable, an address is outside user space, or
the word
.Dv FUTEX_WAKE_OP
changes is not writable.
.It Er EINTR
A handler ended a wait with a timeout, or one without
.Dv SA_RESTART
ended a wait without one.
.It Er EINVAL
PI and ordinary waiters were mixed, the requeue destination equals its
source, a requeue waiter named another destination, a PI requeue's
.Fa val
is not 1, or
.Fa uaddr
is not aligned, a bitset operation was given a zero
.Fa val3 ,
.Fa timeout
has a negative field or nanoseconds past 999999999, a wake, wait or
requeue found a PI waiter or one of
.Dv FUTEX_WAIT_REQUEUE_PI
where it may not, a requeue count is negative, or
.Nm set_robust_list
was given a length other than the head's size.
For
.Nm futex_waitv :
.Fa flags
is not 0,
.Fa nr_futexes
is 0 or past 128,
.Fa waiters
is NULL,
.Fa clockid
is another clock with a
.Fa timeout ,
or an entry's size is not 32 bits, its flags have an unknown bit, its
reserved word is not 0, its value does not fit 32 bits or its address is
not aligned to four bytes.
For the futex2 calls: their flags are refused as an entry's are, a
.Fa mask
is 0 or past 32 bits, a value is past 32 bits, the
.Fa flags
of
.Nm futex_requeue
are not 0 or its
.Fa waiters
is NULL.
.It Er ENOMEM
.Nm futex_waitv
could not have the memory for its waiters.
.It Er EPERM
A PI unlock names another owner, a PI lock names a kernel thread, or
.Nm get_robust_list
named a thread of another user, or a privileged thread of another
process when the caller is not privileged.
.It Er ENOSYS
The operation is not listed above,
.Dv FUTEX_WAKE_OP
was given an unknown operation or comparison, or
.Dv FUTEX_CLOCK_REALTIME
was given to an operation other than
.Dv FUTEX_WAIT_BITSET ,
.Dv FUTEX_WAIT_REQUEUE_PI
or
.Dv FUTEX_LOCK_PI2 .
.It Er ESRCH
There is no current process, a PI word names no live owner, or
.Nm get_robust_list
named no live thread.
.It Er ETIMEDOUT
The deadline passed first.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr clone 2 ,
.Xr nanosleep 2 ,
.Xr sigaction 2
